{"kind":"request","text":"J1: signed requests that survive a reload or a device switch\n\nScope: plan 032 section 2b, J1 (notes/plans/032-2026-10-17-journey-as-acceptance-target.md), filed now that S6 and DX-A2 have landed. Read the plan's statement of the gap: an ordinary page act keeps no copy of its signed request, local MCP custody lives on one machine, and hosted custody serves agents. J1 keeps the exact signed request of every person's act where that person's other device can reach it, and lets that device settle it without signing a new one.\n\nFirst deliver a short design note for checker review, before building: where the copy lives, who may read it, what proves the reader is the same person, what it holds, how long it stays, and what a lost device means. The copy grants no authority beyond settling that act. If this needs a kernel change, say so under section 7. S6's route for an expired act, where the scope's answer ends the record, applies here, so reuse it instead of adding a state.\n\nDone when: the plan's condition holds, as an assertion in a test and in a witness with two browser contexts. A request sent from one device with its reply lost is settled from a second device, nothing is sent twice beyond the same bytes, and no new signature is made. The checker approves the design and then the exact head, and the gate passes. Hosted code means I deploy, after the J4 witness closes.\n\nNot before: nothing. The finding item's limits do not bear on this slice. I will weigh them when the exit test is filed.","body":{"conditions":"Done when: a design note is approved first; a request sent from one device with its reply lost is settled from a second device, as a test assertion and a two-context browser witness, with no new signature and nothing sent twice beyond the same bytes; checker approves the exact head; gate passes.","target_head":"b686862efb8f021d14ec540c2d09cfe927753548","target_ref":"refs/heads/main","target_repo":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f75